BT /F2 15.00 Tf 0.100 0.100 0.120 rg 1 0 0 1 58.00 750.00 Tm (Slide 16 - Horizontal vs vertical scaling) Tj ET
0.040 0.520 1.000 rg 58.00 733.00 496.00 1.40 re f
BT /F2 9.50 Tf 0.420 0.420 0.460 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 717.00 Tm (ON SCREEN) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 701.00 Tm (Two columns - vertical scaling \(one bigger server\) versus horizontal scaling \(more servers behind a load) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 687.50 Tm (balancer\).) Tj ET
0.720 0.720 0.760 rg 58.00 674.00 3.00 49.00 re f
BT /F2 9.50 Tf 0.040 0.340 0.780 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 663.50 Tm (SAY \(read this aloud\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 647.50 Tm (Let's talk about handling more traffic - what we call scaling. There are two ways to do it, and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 632.30 Tm (difference is simple once you picture it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 612.10 Tm (The first is vertical scaling - scaling up. This means taking your one machine and making it more) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 596.90 Tm (powerful - more memory, more CPU. It is simple, but it has a ceiling - you cannot keep adding) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 581.70 Tm (power forever, there is a biggest machine you can buy. And big machines get very expensive.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 566.50 Tm (Plus, it is still just one machine - one point of failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 546.30 Tm (The second is horizontal scaling - scaling out. This means adding more machines, each running a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 531.10 Tm (copy of the same service, with a traffic director in front of them. There is no real ceiling - just keep) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 515.90 Tm (adding machines. It is cost-effective, because you use many ordinary machines instead of one) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 500.70 Tm (giant one. And it is naturally fault-tolerant - if one machine dies, the others carry on.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 480.50 Tm (Think of it like a shop checkout. Vertical scaling is training your one cashier to work faster - there is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 465.30 Tm (a limit. Horizontal scaling is just opening more checkout counters.) Tj ET
0.040 0.520 1.000 rg 58.00 450.10 3.00 219.40 re f
BT /F2 9.50 Tf 0.040 0.340 0.780 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 441.30 Tm (EMPHASISE) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 425.30 Tm (Connect it forward to set up Session 4: microservices are designed for horizontal scaling. Because) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 410.10 Tm (each service is small and remembers nothing between requests, you can spin up as many copies) Tj ET
BT /F1 11.00 Tf 0.120 0.120 0.140 rg 1 0 0 1 72.00 394.90 Tm (as you need and spread traffic across them - which is exactly how the cloud works.) Tj ET
0.040 0.520 1.000 rg 58.00 379.70 3.00 67.60 re f
